  <span class="genre-pill">Literary Fiction</span>
  <h1>The Other Side</h1>
  <div class="story-meta">April 2026</div>
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 2 of 2</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/the-other-side?page=1#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><span class="page-next disabled">Next &#8594;</span></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>David died on a Sunday. Michael was there. He had been there for five days, sleeping in the waiting room and eating hospital food and sitting with his brother for hours that went by in a way that time goes by when someone is dying — slowly, and then all at once, and then not at all.</p>
<p>The last conversation was short. David was tired. He had been tired for days. He was ready.</p>
<p>"I'm sorry," Michael said. "For what I did. For what I didn't do. For all of it."</p>
<p>"I know. I was sorry too. For what I did. For what I didn't do."</p>
<p>The silence was different this time. It was not the silence from 2012. It was something else — something that included the years and the hurt and the waiting and the not-speaking, and that also included the five days in the hospital and the conversation and the understanding that had come too late but had come.</p>
<p>It was not forgiveness. It was something else. Michael didn't have a name for it.</p>
<p>But it was enough.</p>
<p>Michael drove home on Monday. Nine hundred miles, again, in the other direction. He thought about his brother. He thought about the fourteen years and the thing that had started them and the thing that had ended them and the distance between the two that had never been about the thing they thought it was about.</p>
<p>The thing they thought it was about was the secret, the thing David had done, the thing Michael had told. That was the story they had both been telling themselves for fourteen years.</p>
<p>The thing it was actually about was simpler and harder: two people who had loved each other and who had been unable to speak to each other because they didn't know how to hold the hurt without breaking.</p>
<p>Michael drove. He thought about calling someone — his wife, his kids, anyone. He didn't. He just drove, through the landscape, through the time, through the thing that had happened and the thing that was over and the thing that would never come back.</p>
<p>He was not the same person who had made the phone call a week ago. He was not sure who he was now. He was someone who had lost a brother and found him again and lost him again, in the space of a week, and who was carrying all of it — the years and the silence and the last days and the forgiveness that was not quite forgiveness.</p>
<p>He drove home. He arrived on Tuesday evening. He went inside. He held his family.</p>
<p>He did not know what to say to them.</p>
<p>He was not sure he needed to say anything.</p>
<p>Some things are known without being spoken. Some silences hold more than words can hold. The years between him and David had been full of silence, but the silence had been full of knowing, and the knowing had been enough to bring him back for five days and enough to let him be there when David died.</p>
<p>And that, he thought, was what they had.</p>
<p>That was what there was.</p>
<p>He stood in his kitchen, in his house, in his life.</p>
<p>He let the silence be what it was.</p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
